    "slug": "research-education",
    "doc": "---\nname: research-education\ndescription: \"Deep-research methodology for education — study-design hierarchy, effect-size and sample-size caution, and context-transfer limits. Use for education or learning research questions that need cross-context evidence synthesis.\"\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  author: aqsha\n  version: \"1.0\"\n  scope: builtin\n  triggerKeywords: [pendidikan, pembelajaran, desain, studi, effect, size, ukuran, sampel, kuasi-eksperimen, transfer, konteks, kurikulum]\n---\n## Study-design hierarchy\nWeight: meta-analysis/systematic review > randomized experiment (educational RCT) > quasi-experiment (with a comparison group) > correlational > case/qualitative study. For learning-intervention effectiveness claims, prefer designs with a control group.\n\n## Effect size & sample\nReport the effect size (Cohen's d, Hedges' g), not just significance. Beware small samples and the wrong unit of analysis (student vs. class vs. school — mind clustering/design effects). Results from a single class/school are not generalized without qualification.\n\n## Context-transfer limits\nEducation findings are highly context-dependent (curriculum, culture, language, resource level). An intervention that worked in one system may not transfer to the Indonesian context. State context assumptions explicitly.\n\n## Interpretation\nSeparate proxy outcomes (short-term test scores) from meaningful ones (retention, transfer, long-term results). Avoid causal claims from correlational designs; flag publication bias when only positive results are available.\n",
    "resources": []
