    "slug": "research-medicine",
    "doc": "---\nname: research-medicine\ndescription: \"Deep-research methodology for medicine and health — PRISMA screening, evidence hierarchy (meta-analysis > RCT > cohort > case-control), and retraction / predatory-journal flagging. Use for clinical or biomedical research questions that need evidence synthesis.\"\nlicense: Proprietary\nmetadata:\n  author: aqsha\n  version: \"1.0\"\n  scope: builtin\n  triggerKeywords: [kedokteran, klinis, biomedis, kesehatan, prisma, skrining, hierarki, bukti, rct, kohort, meta-analisis, retraction, jurnal, predator]\n---\n## Evidence hierarchy\nWeight sources by study design: systematic review/meta-analysis > randomized controlled trial (RCT) > cohort > case-control > case series > expert opinion. For intervention-efficacy claims, prefer RCTs and meta-analyses; use cohorts only for exposure/prognosis when an RCT is not ethical/feasible.\n\n## PRISMA screening\nTrack the identification → screening → eligibility → inclusion flow. Record explicit inclusion/exclusion criteria (population, intervention, comparator, outcome — PICO) and the study count at each stage so the synthesis is traceable.\n\n## Red flags\nFlag explicitly: retracted articles (check Retraction Watch/PubPeer), predatory journals (not indexed, fast paid processing without real peer review), industry-funding conflicts of interest, and very small samples. Note statistical discrepancies or high heterogeneity (large I²) rather than discarding them.\n\n## Interpretation\nSeparate statistical significance from clinical relevance (effect size, NNT/NNH, confidence intervals). Do not extrapolate in-vitro/animal findings to humans without qualification. State uncertainty when evidence is thin rather than forcing a clinical recommendation.\n",
